      "rationale": "sqlparse/sql.py's Statement.get_type() has an inverted condition (`elif token.ttype != T.Keyword.CTE:` instead of `== T.Keyword.CTE`) guarding the branch that scans past a WITH clause's CTE definitions to find the trailing DML keyword; as a result any statement starting with WITH now falls straight through to `return 'UNKNOWN'`, which is exactly the reported `AssertionError: assert 'UNKNOWN' == 'SELECT'`. The first two tests reproduce the two named regressed checks (a simple CTE, and a CTE list with an inline comment between clauses, per the documented behavior in get_type()'s own docstring that \"the actual DML keyword ... will follow next\"), and the third confirms ordinary non-CTE statements keep returning the correct type so the fix doesn't introduce a regression elsewhere.",
